[Unit]
Description=Delete /mnt/boot/ga-ethernet-force (device is converged; the fleet-manager owns this switch now)
# The link is deliberately NOT reconciled here — see `do_retire` in
# ga-manage-ethernet. The device is provisioned over this very interface.
#
# NO ConditionPathExists ON THIS UNIT. It carried one, and that is what turned
# a one-shot into a hot loop: .path re-activates its unit as soon as that unit
# goes inactive while the watched path still exists, and .ga_converged exists
# for the rest of the device's life. A unit skipped by an unmet condition goes
# inactive immediately, so the pair re-triggered each other as fast as PID 1
# could schedule them — pinning a core and writing the same "skipped because of
# an unmet condition check" line to the journal many times a second, forever,
# on every converged device. `do_retire` is already idempotent ("nothing to
# retire"), so the guard bought nothing and cost that.
#
# The loop is broken by disarming the trigger instead: the watcher exists for
# exactly one event per device lifetime, so it stops itself once it has fired.
# --no-block, because a blocking stop of the very unit that started us would
# wait on our own job. `-` on the prefix: failing to disarm must not mark the
# retire itself as failed — the marker is already gone by then.

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/ga-manage-ethernet retire
ExecStartPost=-/usr/bin/systemctl --no-block stop ga-ethernet-retire.path
RemainAfterExit=no
